  annotation = {中文辅助译名：结合催眠和虚拟自然暴露对患者进行干预的可行性和初步效果造血细胞移植},
  abstract = {Abstract Introduction. Hematopoietic cell transplantation (HCT) is associated with physical symptoms, psychological distress, and cognitive fatigue. Hypnosis has demonstrated benefits in oncology but typically requires cognitive resources underlying active mental imagery. Virtual reality (VR) could reduce this cognitive load by providing structured visual inputs. This study evaluated the feasibility and preliminary efficacy of an intervention combining hypnosis with virtual nature exposure in patients undergoing HCT. Methods. A randomized longitudinal feasibility trial was conducted. Participants in the experimental group used an application offering virtual nature environments and hypnosis for two weeks. Feasibility outcomes included recruitment, attrition, usability, cybersickness, and qualitative user experience interviews. Main outcome of interest for preliminary efficacy was quality of life. Immediate pre-post session effects on pain, anxiety, and relaxation were examined using visual numeric scales. Results. Forty participants were enrolled. Application use averaged 2.8 sessions over two weeks, and cybersickness was minimal. Qualitative feedback indicated positive user experience, with perceived benefits for anxiety. Immediate effects included significant reductions in fatigue and anxiety and increase in relaxation and amusement after the first session. However, no significant group effect was observed for outcomes at 1 and 3 months. Conclusion. The intervention appears},
}

@misc{karim_jerbi_f97cdbc42ce9,
  title = {Resting-state neural oscillations predict individual differences in verbal learning and encoding strategy use},
  author = {Victor Oswald and Mathieu Landry and Hamza Abdelhedi and Sarah Lippé and Philippe Robaey and Karim Jerbi},
  year = {2026},
  doi = {10.1016/j.isci.2026.117377},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.117377},
  howpublished = {iScience},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：静息态神经振荡预测言语学习和编码策略使用中的个体差异},
  abstract = {Individuals adopt different encoding strategies to facilitate learning, yet few studies have examined the neurophysiological basis of these strategies across individuals. The present work addresses this gap by extending our previous findings on the direct relationship between cortical spectral power, measured via resting-state magnetoencephalography, and standard cognitive performance, to test whether resting-state neural features predict individual differences in encoding strategy preferences. Our results highlight the complex interactions between endogenous brain oscillations, learning, and verbal encoding strategies assessed by the California Verbal Learning Test-Second Edition (CVLT-2). First, resting-state theta oscillations were significantly associated with verbal learning and subjective clustering strategies. Second, semantic clustering was facilitated by oscillatory patterns in the left sensory-motor regions. Finally, serial and semantic clustering strategies showed opposite regression patterns, indicating a competitive interaction. Together, these findings provide insights into resting-state neural markers associated with diverse encoding strategies in verbal learning.},
}

@misc{karim_jerbi_8d19a9b4aab3,
